==================================================================
DAY 44 - PART B: LAB
Create, explore, and delete an EKS cluster
Time: 90 minutes (cluster creation alone takes ~15-20 minutes)
==================================================================

STEP 1: Create the cluster
--------------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey/cloud/eks
time eksctl create cluster -f cluster.yaml

While it runs (15-20 min), open the AWS console:
  CloudFormation -> watch the two stacks being created
  VPC -> the new VPC, its subnets, NAT gateway, route tables
  EKS -> cluster "lab"


STEP 2: Connect
---------------
eksctl wrote your kubeconfig. Check:
  kubectx
  k config current-context
  k get nodes -o wide
  k get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone,node.kubernetes.io/instance-type,eks.amazonaws.com/nodegroup

If needed: aws eks update-kubeconfig --region ap-south-1 --name lab


STEP 3: What's running?
-----------------------
k get pods -A -o wide
k get ds -n kube-system
k get deploy -n kube-system
eksctl get addons --cluster lab

Note: aws-node (VPC CNI), kube-proxy, coredns, ebs-csi-*,
eks-pod-identity-agent. NO apiserver/etcd/scheduler pods.


STEP 4: Pods get VPC IPs
------------------------
k create deploy web --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=4
k get pods -o wide
aws ec2 describe-subnets --filters "Name=tag:alpha.eksctl.io/cluster-name,Values=lab" \
  --query 'Subnets[].{AZ:AvailabilityZone,CIDR:CidrBlock,Free:AvailableIpAddressCount}' --output table

Pod IPs are from the private subnets' ranges, the same network as
the nodes. Check how many pods a t3.medium can run:
  k get node -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.allocatable.pods}{"\n"}'     # 17 (ENI/IP limit)


STEP 5: Who am I in Kubernetes?
-------------------------------
aws sts get-caller-identity
k auth whoami
aws eks list-access-entries --cluster-name lab
k config view --minify | grep -A10 exec:


STEP 6: Spread across zones
---------------------------
k scale deploy web --replicas=6
k get pods -o wide
k get nodes -L topology.kubernetes.io/zone
Add a topologySpreadConstraint with topologyKey
topology.kubernetes.io/zone to the Deployment (k edit deploy web) and
check that replicas are balanced between zones.


STEP 7: Scale the node group
----------------------------
eksctl get nodegroup --cluster lab
eksctl scale nodegroup --cluster lab --name ng-1 --nodes 3
k get nodes -w          # new node joins in 2-3 minutes
eksctl scale nodegroup --cluster lab --name ng-1 --nodes 2


STEP 8: Look at it in the console
---------------------------------
EKS console -> lab -> Resources / Compute / Networking / Add-ons /
Access. EC2 console -> Instances (your nodes) and Auto Scaling Groups.


STEP 9: DELETE THE CLUSTER
--------------------------
k delete deploy web
eksctl delete cluster -f cluster.yaml --wait
Check in the console: CloudFormation stacks deleted, no EC2
instances, no NAT gateway, no Elastic IPs left.
  aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways --query 'NatGateways[?State!=`deleted`].NatGatewayId'
  aws ec2 describe-addresses --query 'Addresses[].PublicIp'


STEP 10: AKS / GKE equivalents
------------------------------
AKS: az aks create ... ; az aks get-credentials ... ; az aks nodepool scale
     -g lab-rg --cluster-name lab -n nodepool1 -c 3 ; az group delete
GKE: gcloud container clusters create ... ; gcloud container clusters resize
     lab --node-pool default-pool --num-nodes 2 --region asia-south1 ; delete

cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day44: first eks cluster"
